[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Transport Layer Security (TLS) and SQL Injection Prevention in MySQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing a MySQL database requires a defense-in-depth strategy. This tutorial covers two critical pillars of database security: encrypting data in transit using Transport Layer Security (TLS) and mitigating SQL Injection vulnerabilities through parameterized queries in application code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Part 1: Configuring TLS in MySQL"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transport Layer Security (TLS) ensures that all data transferred between the MySQL server and the client remains encrypted and tamper-proof. Modern MySQL installations generate SSL/auto-certificates by default, but production environments require custom certificates."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generate a Certificate Authority (CA), server certificate, and client certificate using OpenSSL."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure the MySQL server configuration file (my.cnf or my.ini) to point to these keys."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"[mysqld]
ssl-ca=/path/to/ca.pem
ssl-cert=/path/to/server-cert.pem
ssl-key=/path/to/server-key.pem
require_secure_transport=ON"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Setting require_secure_transport to ON enforces that all client connections must use encryption, rejecting any unencrypted attempts."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Part 2: Verifying TLS Connections"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"After enforcing TLS on the server, you can verify that clients are connecting securely by executing a status command inside the MySQL client shell."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"mysql -u app_user -p --ssl-ca=/path/to/ca.pem

-- Once logged in, run:
SHOW STATUS LIKE 'Ssl_cipher';"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"If the query returns a valid cipher suite instead of being empty, your connection is successfully encrypted using TLS."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Part 3: Preventing SQL Injection"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"While TLS protects data while it is moving across the network, it does nothing to prevent malicious SQL statements from being executed on the database server. SQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is directly concatenated into a SQL query string."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the following vulnerable Node.js/MySQL example using direct string concatenation:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"const userInput = "admin' OR '1'='1";
// VULNERABLE CODE:
const query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + userInput + "';";
db.query(query, (err, results) => { /* ... */ });"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Part 4: Implementing Prepared Statements"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To completely neutralize SQL Injection, you must use parameterized queries (prepared statements). This ensures that the database driver treats user input strictly as data, never as executable SQL code."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"const userInput = "admin";
// SECURE CODE:
const query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
db.query(query, [userInput], (err, results) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log(results);
});"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining TLS for secure network transit and prepared statements for secure query execution, you establish a robust security baseline for your MySQL database architecture."}]
